# AGENTS.md

Guidance for AI coding agents working on {{.AppName}}, a Go REST API generated by
[GoForge](https://github.com/Frank5ive/goforge). People are welcome to read it too.

## Use GoForge for the infrastructure

GoForge generated this project and can extend it. Use it instead of writing these by hand:

- **Add a REST resource** (model, validation, storage, service, handlers, tests, and routes):
  `goforge add resource <name> --fields "field:type,..."`. Types are `string`, `int`, `float`,
  `bool`, and `time`; a trailing `?` makes a string optional, e.g. `image_url:string?`.
- **Store data in a database:** `goforge add db <postgres|mysql|sqlite|mongo>`.
- **Understand the project:** `goforge inspect --json` lists its resources and fields, routes,
  database, and environment variables.
- **Check your work:** `goforge check --json` runs gofmt, go vet, the tests, and golangci-lint
  (when it is installed; otherwise the report marks lint as skipped), and reports each problem
  with its file and line. Fix every problem before you finish.

`generate`, `add resource`, `add db`, `inspect`, and `check` accept `--json` (a machine-readable
result) and never wait for input. `generate` and the `add` commands also accept `--dry-run` (a
preview) and change all files or none.
Install GoForge with `go install github.com/Frank5ive/goforge@latest`.

## Rules

- Keep the `// goforge:` and `# goforge:` marker comments. GoForge inserts code above them; without
  them, `goforge add` can't update the file.
- Don't edit `.goforge.json` by hand; `goforge add` keeps it up to date.
- Put business rules in the resource's package: validation in `Input.Validate` (in `<resource>.go`)
  and use cases in `service.go`. Handlers only translate between HTTP and the service.
- For client errors, return (or wrap) the resource's `ErrNotFound` or `ErrInvalid`. Any other error
  becomes a 500 that is logged with its cause.
- Log with `handlers.Logger(ctx)`, so log lines carry the request ID.
- Add settings to `config.Config` and load them in `config/config.go`, reporting invalid values
  with the others rather than failing on the first.
- Keep tests next to the code. Each resource's `repository_test.go` runs the same tests against
  every storage implementation, so a new repository must pass them.

## Layout

- `main.go`: loads the configuration, connects dependencies, and runs the server
- `config/`: settings from environment variables
- `server/`: routes (`routes.go`), middleware, and shared dependencies (`deps.go`)
- `handlers/`: health and readiness checks, and JSON helpers (`WriteJSON`, `WriteError`,
  `DecodeJSON`, `InternalError`, `Logger`){{if .IncludeLogger}}
- `logger/`: structured logging setup{{end}}
- one package per resource, e.g. `products/`
- `db/`: the database connection and migrations, once a database is added

## Run and test

- `make run` starts the server in development mode (`APP_ENV=development`), where a 500 response
  includes its cause in `detail`; plain `go run .` uses production mode, without it. Error
  responses from the handlers have a `request_id` that matches the `X-Request-ID` header and the
  server's log lines. (Unknown routes and unsupported methods get Go's plain-text 404 and 405,
  which still carry the header.)
- `go test ./...` runs the tests. With a database in Docker, `make db-up` starts it and
  `make test-db` runs the database tests too.
- `make help` lists every Makefile target.
